Let A={n²:n∈N} and B={n³:n∈N}
A={1,4,9,16,…..}
and B={1,8,27,64,……}
Now, A∩B={1} which is a finite set.
Also, A∪B={1,4,8,9,27,…..}
So, complement of A∪B is infinite set. Hence, A∪B≠N

Let A={1,2}
B={3,4,0}
C={5,6,0}
D={7,8}
Such that (A∩B)=(C∩D)=ϕ
⇒(A∪C)={1,2,5,6,0}
⇒(B∪D)={3,4,7,8,0}
⇒(A∪C)∩(B∪D)={0}
So (A∪C) and (B∪D) are not always disjoint ⇒(A∩C)=ϕ and (B∩D)=ϕ
So (A∩C) and (B∩D) are always disjoint.

